WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:06.827
Back in the warehouse, a cleaning crew keeps the floor from filling up.

2
00:00:06.827 --> 00:00:09.709
And the crew has a rule that sounds a little rude.

3
00:00:09.709 --> 00:00:12.823
It never asks the workers which boxes they still need.

4
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:13.825
Fair enough.

5
00:00:13.825 --> 00:00:17.466
Ask any worker, and you know the answer: all of them.

6
00:00:17.466 --> 00:00:18.634
Every single box.

7
00:00:18.634 --> 00:00:20.695
I might need that one someday.

8
00:00:20.695 --> 00:00:22.825
So the crew decides for itself.

9
00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:27.548
Which brings us to a question that's easy to be shy about.

10
00:00:27.548 --> 00:00:31.426
How does the garbage collector know I'm done with an object?

11
00:00:31.426 --> 00:00:32.460
I never told it.

12
00:00:33.533 --> 00:00:35.255
Here's how the crew decides.

13
00:00:35.255 --> 00:00:39.435
It starts at the workers' desks, and at the noticeboard by the door.

14
00:00:39.435 --> 00:00:42.386
It follows every shelf number written on a note.

15
00:00:42.386 --> 00:00:45.091
Then every note it finds inside those boxes.

16
00:00:45.091 --> 00:00:46.874
It keeps whatever it reaches.

17
00:00:46.874 --> 00:00:48.165
Everything else goes.

18
00:00:48.333 --> 00:00:49.148
Let's map it.

19
00:00:49.148 --> 00:00:53.409
The desks are the threads' stacks, where their local variables live.

20
00:00:53.409 --> 00:00:55.727
The noticeboard is the static fields.

21
00:00:55.727 --> 00:01:00.427
Together, with references held by native code, they're called the GC roots.

22
00:01:00.427 --> 00:01:03.059
A note with a shelf number is a reference.

23
00:01:03.059 --> 00:01:05.440
And the crew is the garbage collector.

24
00:01:06.333 --> 00:01:07.182
Now a puzzle.

25
00:01:07.182 --> 00:01:10.057
Three boxes point at each other in a circle.

26
00:01:10.057 --> 00:01:12.735
Each one is still referred to by another.

27
00:01:12.735 --> 00:01:16.915
But no note from a desk or the noticeboard leads to any of them.

28
00:01:16.915 --> 00:01:17.830
Are they kept?

29
00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:18.875
Pause and guess.

30
00:01:21.933 --> 00:01:22.482
They go.

31
00:01:22.482 --> 00:01:25.086
The collector never counts references.

32
00:01:25.086 --> 00:01:28.170
It only asks one thing: can a root reach you?

33
00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:35.229
A box that no root's chain of notes leads to is garbage, even if a hundred forgotten boxes point at it.

34
00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:39.435
So the garbage collector doesn't free the objects you stopped using.

35
00:01:39.435 --> 00:01:41.749
It frees the objects nothing can reach.

36
00:01:42.867 --> 00:01:47.886
Java also lets you hold an object more loosely than a plain reference does.

37
00:01:47.886 --> 00:01:52.035
The package java.lang.ref has three kinds of reference object.

38
00:01:52.035 --> 00:01:58.794
Each one points at another object, its referent, and get returns the referent while it's still there.

39
00:01:58.794 --> 00:02:01.069
Picture them as rungs on a ladder.

40
00:02:01.267 --> 00:02:04.840
At the top, strong: a chain of ordinary references.

41
00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:06.592
The crew leaves it alone.

42
00:02:06.667 --> 00:02:09.713
Soft: the only way in goes through a SoftReference.

43
00:02:09.713 --> 00:02:11.743
It means: keep it if there's room.

44
00:02:11.743 --> 00:02:14.729
The collector may clear it when memory gets tight.

45
00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:17.495
Weak: keep it only while someone else holds it.

46
00:02:17.495 --> 00:02:20.019
The next collection that finds it clears it.

47
00:02:20.133 --> 00:02:20.952
And phantom.

48
00:02:20.952 --> 00:02:22.863
Its get always returns null.

49
00:02:22.863 --> 00:02:26.617
It only tells you, afterwards, that the object is gone.

50
00:02:27.733 --> 00:02:36.172
The lesson's ReferenceLadderDemo puts one 1,000-byte array on each rung, then asks for a collection with System.gc.

51
00:02:36.172 --> 00:02:37.566
Which ones survive?

52
00:02:37.566 --> 00:02:38.740
Pause and guess.

53
00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:43.075
Here's the output.

54
00:02:43.075 --> 00:02:47.185
Before: soft true, weak true, and phantom null, as always.

55
00:02:47.185 --> 00:02:51.011
After: soft is still true, because memory isn't tight.

56
00:02:51.011 --> 00:02:52.003
Weak is false.

57
00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:59.494
And the queue, a tray where the collector drops a slip for each reference it cleared, gets the weak one and the phantom one.

58
00:02:59.494 --> 00:03:01.259
The strong array is untouched.

59
00:03:02.133 --> 00:03:05.731
Here the warehouse picture breaks, and the break matters.

60
00:03:05.731 --> 00:03:08.319
A real cleaning crew comes on a schedule.

61
00:03:08.319 --> 00:03:16.460
The JVM's crew comes when the heap needs room: many times a second on a busy service, and perhaps not for an hour on a quiet one.

62
00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:18.480
Nothing on the ladder says when.

63
00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:23.055
That missing when is what hurts soft references.

64
00:03:23.055 --> 00:03:25.188
Here's the lesson's squeeze.

65
00:03:25.188 --> 00:03:30.595
A 64 MB heap keeps a 40 MB decoded beach photo behind a soft reference.

66
00:03:30.595 --> 00:03:33.869
Then the service decodes a 40 MB dog photo.

67
00:03:34.067 --> 00:03:40.669
G1, the default collector, runs a full collection, with every thread stopped, and keeps the soft photo.

68
00:03:40.669 --> 00:03:42.079
There's still no room.

69
00:03:42.079 --> 00:03:47.656
So it runs a second full collection, clears the soft reference, and the dog photo fits.

70
00:03:47.656 --> 00:03:51.053
The cache is gone, right when the machine is busiest.

71
00:03:51.933 --> 00:03:56.296
A soft cache is a friend who holds your umbrella until it starts to rain.

72
00:03:56.296 --> 00:04:03.467
So the point is this: it keeps your things until the moment you need them most, and then lets go of all of them at once.

73
00:04:04.467 --> 00:04:07.182
Weak references have the opposite problem.

74
00:04:07.182 --> 00:04:12.999
A weak cache forgets at the next collection, which on a busy service is milliseconds away.

75
00:04:12.999 --> 00:04:20.304
Weak references do a different job well: attaching data to an object someone else owns, without keeping it alive.

76
00:04:20.304 --> 00:04:22.179
That's what WeakHashMap does.

77
00:04:23.067 --> 00:04:24.161
So three rules.

78
00:04:24.161 --> 00:04:29.923
For a cache, use one with a size limit and an expiry time, not soft references.

79
00:04:29.923 --> 00:04:35.831
Use weak references for data attached to objects you don't own, never for caches.

80
00:04:35.831 --> 00:04:41.739
And System.gc is a request, not a command: fine in a demo, never in service code.

81
00:04:42.667 --> 00:04:45.776
So, how does the garbage collector know you're done?

82
00:04:45.776 --> 00:04:46.433
It doesn't.

83
00:04:46.433 --> 00:04:51.814
It knows what it can still reach from the roots, and frees the rest, at a time it chooses.

84
00:04:51.814 --> 00:04:53.488
Never plan around that time.

85
00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:55.168
Quick check.

86
00:04:55.168 --> 00:05:01.311
A cache is a Map of String to WeakReference of Picture, and nothing else refers to the pictures.

87
00:05:01.311 --> 00:05:03.615
How long does a cached picture last?

88
00:05:06.867 --> 00:05:09.326
Until the next collection that finds it.

89
00:05:09.326 --> 00:05:12.277
The map entry stays, but its reference is empty.
